  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nCloud cover has been slow to erode thanks to an MCS over Iowa this \nmorning that is now over WI and IL and continuing eastward. \nSubsidence behind this feature has allowed for cloud cover in the \nnorthern half of the state to erode allowing for partly cloudy \nskies. This area of subsidence is expected to continue southward \nacross MN and WI but timing could limit the amount of observed \nsunshine across southern MN the rest of today. Thus surface high \ntemperatures will be a warmer mainly northeast of I-94 near 80 \ndegrees while southern MN will remain in the lower 70s. Two bands of \nrain showers continue to linger on radar this afternoon but as the \naforementioned mid-level dry air moves in, this should weaken any \nprecip activity later this afternoon. Then we go into a short\nhiatus from precip tonight leaving forecast lows in the lower \n60s for most.\n\nFriday into the weekend...a shortwave wave on the western region of \nan upper-level ridge creates a weak surface low in the northern \nRocky Mountain Piedmont's early Friday morning. This low will \ntrack NE into the northern plains Friday afternoon. A surface \nwarm front stalls mainly over during the daytime hours which \nshould keep southern MN under a better potential for seeing any \nrain and storms at least for the first half of the day. However,\nwe will also need to keep an eye on the progression of another \nwave embedded in zonal flow aloft that will be moving across \nthe Dakotas later on Friday into Saturday. This will aid in the \nexpansion of some precip chances northwestward but timing \ndifferences make precip chances less certain thus highest \nconfidence for precip remains south of I-94. QPF continues to \ntrend down. As of now, the area that looks to favor closer to an\ninch is south of a line which extends from Red Wing, MN down to\nSt. James, MN. Areas north of this line into central MN and \nwestern WI, could see near tenth to quarter of an inch of rain \nby Saturday night. As mentioned in the previous discussion, the\nrain is always welcomed but will do little to improve current \ndrought conditions. As for temperatures, dew points will become \na bit sticker on Friday into Saturday as they creep into the \nupper 60s and perhaps lower 70s before retreating back into near\n60 on Sunday. Forecast highs through this period will range in \nthe upper 70s to lower 80s. \n\nBetween Saturday night into early next week upper-level ridging \nwhich has been building in the southeastern U.S. will take hold over \nmuch of the country keeping Sunday and Monday's skies mostly clear. \nBy Monday night the forecast pattern will remain under zonal flow \nand begin featuring near daily lower-end precip chances. As of now, \nthe better signal for a more persistent rain doesnt look to arrive \nuntil late Tuesday night into Wednesday. Temperatures for the \nremainder of the period remain near-normal.",
